[section:design Design Notes]

This chapter records the design decisions that are not obvious from the
reference documentation alone.

* [link boost_taar.design.why_header_only Why header-only?]: what would
  be different if Taar were a compiled library.
* [link boost_taar.design.tag_invoke Tag invoke as the extension story]:
  why `tag_invoke` overloads instead of virtual functions or template
  specialisations.
* [link boost_taar.design.soft_vs_hard Soft errors vs hard errors]:
  what gets turned into an HTTP response and what tears the connection
  down.
* [link boost_taar.design.matchers Matchers as expression templates]:
  why operator overloading and how it interacts with route dispatch
  cost.
* [link boost_taar.design.async The asynchronous model]: how
  coroutines thread through the session, the handlers, and streaming
  responses.
* [link boost_taar.design.layers Layers, not a framework]: how REST,
  JSON-RPC, MCP and OpenAPI sit on one HTTP transport.
* [link boost_taar.design.contexts Contexts and keyed state]: the three
  scopes of state a handler can ask for, and how `context_store` keeps
  them safe.
* [link boost_taar.design.errors Error mapping per surface]: why domain
  code throws plain exceptions and each layer maps them.
* [link boost_taar.design.protocols Protocol decisions]: the choices made
  where JSON-RPC, MCP and OpenAPI leave room.

[section:why_header_only Why header-only?]

Every public type in Taar is a template that instantiates differently for
each user-supplied handler signature, request body, response body, and
executor. There is no implementation that would benefit from being
precompiled into a library: separating declaration from definition would
force users to instantiate every combination explicitly. A header-only
distribution keeps the build simple and matches the rest of the
matcher/handler ecosystem on top of Boost.Beast and Boost.Asio.

[endsect]

[section:tag_invoke Tag invoke as the extension story]

The two main customisation points, `response_from` and `rest_arg_cast`,
are exposed as `tag_invoke` overloads rather than virtual functions or
class template specialisations.

`tag_invoke` is found by ordinary argument-dependent lookup, which means a
user-defined return type or argument type can opt in to Taar by declaring a
free function next to the type itself, without touching any namespace owned
by the library. This is the same pattern used by `boost::json::value_from`
and the C++26 sender/receiver proposal.

[endsect]

[section:soft_vs_hard Soft errors vs hard errors]

A /soft error/ is something that an HTTP handler should be able to turn
into a normal HTTP response: a missing query parameter, an unparseable
path argument, a body that fails to deserialise. A /hard error/ is
something that takes the connection down: a peer disconnect mid-read, a
write that fails because the socket is closed.

Both have pluggable handlers installed on a session via
`set_soft_error_handler` and `set_hard_error_handler`. The default soft
handler answers library argument errors with `400` and a short text body,
a streaming handler's body limit with `413`, and rethrows everything else, which then reaches the hard error handler
and closes the connection. Applications that throw their own exceptions
from handlers install a soft error handler that maps them (see
`examples/rest_crud_server.cpp`).

[endsect]

[section:matchers Matchers as expression templates]

A matcher composition like

    method == http::verb::get && target == "/api/{id}" && version == 11

is not evaluated eagerly. Each operator returns a small object that holds
its operands by value and carries the combined matching logic. The result
is one expression-template object per registered route, with no heap
allocation and full inlining at the call site.

This is the same technique that __Boost_Spirit__, __Boost_Hana__, and
__Boost_Proto__ rely on. The cost is that diagnostics for an ill-formed
matcher are longer than they would be with a runtime API; the benefit is
that route dispatch is a flat sequence of `if`s with no virtual calls.

The same expression also builds a type-level description of itself: the
`metadata_type` of the resulting operand is an AST of leaf tags joined by
`and_`, `or_` and `not_`, and the operand stores what each leaf captured
(the verb, the path template, the header value). That is the only
information the OpenAPI generator uses, so matching logic and
documentation can never disagree.

[endsect]

[section:async The asynchronous model]

Taar is asynchronous from top to bottom, built on __Asio__ C++20
coroutines. There is no thread-per-connection pool and no callback
plumbing in user code; concurrency is expressed with `co_await`.

[heading Everything is a coroutine]

The TCP acceptor (`taar::server::tcp`) and the per-connection session
(`taar::session::http`) are both `taar::awaitable<void>` coroutines
spawned with `boost::asio::co_spawn`. One in-flight connection is one
suspended coroutine frame, not a thread, so a single-threaded
`io_context` already serves many simultaneous connections; running the
`io_context` from several threads scales it across cores.

`taar::awaitable<T>` is a thin alias for
`boost::asio::awaitable<T, any_io_executor>`, which spares users from
spelling the second template argument every time and lets the same
coroutine run on a plain `io_context` executor or on a strand. The
servers spawn every connection on its own strand, so a multi-threaded
`io.run()` needs no further care in handlers: one connection's
operations never run concurrently with each other.

[heading Synchronous or asynchronous handlers]

A request handler wrapped by `taar::handler::rest` may return its value
directly /or/ return a `taar::awaitable<T>`. The session detects which
and does the right thing: a plain value is converted to a response
immediately; an awaitable is `co_await`ed first. This means a handler
that needs to talk to a database or call another service simply becomes
a coroutine (its signature changes from `T` to `taar::awaitable<T>` and
its body gains `co_await`/`co_return`) with no change to how it is
registered or how its arguments are extracted.

The soft-error handler (see
[link boost_taar.design.soft_vs_hard Soft errors vs hard errors]) may
likewise be a coroutine, so error responses can also be produced from
asynchronous work.

[heading Streaming responses]

For responses that are produced incrementally (server-sent events, a
large file, the output of a long-running job), a handler can return a
`taar::async_generator<T>` or a `taar::chunked_response<T>`. These are
themselves coroutines: the body `co_yield`s each piece of the response
and may `co_await` between pieces, while `taar::chunked_response`
additionally lets the producer set the HTTP status and headers (by
`co_yield`ing `taar::set_status` / `taar::set_header`) before the first
chunk goes on the wire. The session drives the generator and writes each
yielded value using HTTP chunked transfer-encoding.

Request bodies stream too. A handler whose request type is
`streaming_request` is invoked before the body is read and pulls it
through a `body_reader` straight from the connection, so the session's
body limit is bypassed and memory stays bounded by the handler's buffer. A
body left unread when the response is produced closes the connection
rather than being drained, since draining an arbitrarily large upload to
keep one connection alive is rarely the right trade.

`multipart/form-data` is parsed by one incremental scanner used both for
buffered forms (`parse_multipart`) and for streamed ones
(`multipart_reader`). The scanner holds back only as many bytes as a
delimiter could span, so a file part is delivered in pieces regardless of
how the bytes arrived; a malformed body is an `invalid_request_format`
soft error, and a part without a `name` is treated as malformed.
`multipart_reader::next_part` discards whatever is left of the current
part, so a handler may skip fields it does not care about.

Server-push streams that wait for events produced elsewhere (the MCP
event stream, a chat room) read from a `taar::async_channel<T>`: any
thread pushes, the generator `co_await`s `async_receive`, and `close()`
ends the stream.

[heading Cancellation and graceful shutdown]

Shutdown is cooperative. A `taar::cancellation_signals` pool hands a
cancellation slot to every spawned coroutine; emitting on the pool
(typically from a `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM` handler) stops the acceptor and
cancels the idle read on every keep-alive connection, so idle connections
close at once. Asio requires a cancellation to be emitted within the
strand of the operation it cancels, so the servers register each
connection's slot together with its strand and `emit()` dispatches the
emission there; it may therefore be called from any thread, including
one that is not running the `io_context`. The plain `slot()` form, which
emits inline, remains for single-threaded use. The server coroutine
itself ignores a slot bound by its spawner and stops through the pool.

A request whose handler is running when the signal arrives is allowed to
finish: the session shields a plain (non-streaming) handler from
cancellation, records that shutdown was requested, writes the handler's
response with `Connection: close`, and ends the connection. Streaming
handlers stay cancellable, because an open-ended stream would otherwise
hold the process up; the cancellation propagates into the generator's
pending `co_await` and the connection is dropped.

`basic_http` is parameterised on its stream type. The default is Beast's
`tcp_stream`; `session::https` instantiates it over
`beast::ssl_stream<tcp_stream>`, and `server::tls` owns the handshake so
the session never sees a half-established connection. On exit a TLS
session sends `close_notify` (bounded by a five second timer) before the
TCP half-close. Nothing in the handler layer depends on the stream type,
so every route, context and protocol layer works unchanged over TLS.

A request that matches no route is answered `405 Method Not Allowed`
with an `Allow` header when some route accepts the same path under another
method, and `404` otherwise (including when the method is accepted but a
header, cookie or version constraint rejected the request). `HEAD` is served through a matching `GET`
route: the handler runs with the HEAD request and only the response
header is written, so `Content-Length` is what the GET would carry. Both
behaviours use the matcher metadata (a route must be a conjunction of one
`target ==` and `method ==` constraints to take part) and both can be
switched off with `set_method_not_allowed` and `set_head_from_get`.

Responses are always aligned with the request they answer: same HTTP
version, and keep-alive only if the request allows it, the handler did not
disable it, and the server is not shutting down. Pipelined requests are
served in order from one per-connection buffer. A peer that closes an idle
keep-alive connection is not an error; a generator that throws after
chunks are on the wire truncates the response and reports the exception
to the hard error handler.

[endsect]

[section:layers Layers, not a framework]

The HTTP session, the matchers and `handler::rest` form the transport and
REST layer. JSON-RPC, MCP and OpenAPI are separate objects layered on top
of it rather than modes of the session:

* each layer is user-owned and keeps its own registry, so a program can
  have several JSON-RPC endpoints or none;
* each layer hands the session an ordinary handler for one route, so
  routing, keep-alive, limits, contexts and error handling are shared;
* MCP composes a JSON-RPC endpoint instead of re-implementing it, and
  custom MCP methods are plain JSON-RPC methods;
* REST has no registry object of its own: routes are registered directly
  on the session, and the OpenAPI generator observes those registrations.

The binding machinery is shared too. `jsonrpc::detail::bind_method`
turns a callable plus argument providers into an invoker and a parameter
description; `handler::rest`, JSON-RPC methods, MCP tools and MCP
resources all use the same context-injection rules and the same
`rest_arg_cast` conversions, which is what lets one `int add(calc&, int,
int)` serve all three surfaces unchanged. Providers that read the
HTTP request (`header_arg`, `cookie_arg`, `query_arg`) are accepted on
every surface as well, because every call type carries the request it
arrived on.

[endsect]

[section:contexts Contexts and keyed state]

A handler can ask for state in three scopes by declaring leading
parameters:

* /connection/: `basic_http<T>` constructs one `T` when a connection is
  accepted and destroys it when the connection closes. Keep-alive makes
  this the natural home for per-client counters and caches.
* /keyed/: a `context_store<Key, T>` holds one `T` per key with idle
  expiry; `with_session` resolves the key from a cookie or header
  (minting one when allowed), locks the entry for the duration of the
  request and injects the `T`. Under a streaming handler the lock covers
  only the call that produces the stream; the chunks then flow unlocked,
  so one client's open stream never blocks its other requests, and the
  minted key travels in the stream's headers. The MCP endpoint uses the
  same store for its sessions keyed by `Mcp-Session-Id`.
* /none/: handlers without context parameters work in every scope.

Contexts are matched by type, in any order, so adding a context parameter
never shifts the argument providers. `T&` and `T const&` are preferred;
`std::shared_ptr<T>` is accepted for handlers that need to retain the
object beyond the request.

`context_store` is designed around three facts: requests for the same key
may arrive concurrently on different connections and threads, so each
entry has an asynchronous lock that serialises them while requests for
different keys run in parallel; entries must disappear when clients go
away without telling us, so every access touches a timestamp and sweeps
expired entries opportunistically (no background timer); and a locked or
in-flight entry must never be swept. Keys minted by the store are 128
random bits as hexadecimal, safe in cookies and headers. Set-Cookie uses
`Path=/; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax` by default and never sets an expiry: the
store's idle timeout is the session lifetime.

[endsect]

[section:errors Error mapping per surface]

Domain code throws plain exceptions and knows nothing about HTTP, JSON-RPC
or MCP. Each layer maps exceptions where they cross into its protocol, with
a default and a hook:

[table
[[Surface][Default][Hook]]
[[REST][library argument errors -> `400`; others close the connection]
 [`set_soft_error_handler`]]
[[JSON-RPC][argument errors -> `-32602` with the detail in `data`; unknown
 method -> `-32601`; other exceptions -> `-32603` with `what()`]
 [`set_error_mapper`]]
[[MCP tools][argument errors -> `-32602`; other exceptions -> a result with
 `isError: true` and `what()` as text]
 [`set_tool_error_handler`, then the JSON-RPC mapper]]
]

A throwing mapper falls back to the default, so an error in error handling
cannot take a connection down.

[endsect]

[section:protocols Protocol decisions]

Where the specifications leave room, Taar makes these choices:

[heading JSON-RPC 2.0]

* Every JSON response, including error objects, is HTTP `200`. Only
  notifications (and all-notification batches) answer `202` with an empty
  body.
* Batches are processed element by element; one failure never aborts its
  siblings. An empty batch is a single `-32600` object. An invalid element
  is answered with `-32600` and its `id` if that was a string, number or
  null.
* Parameters bind by name from a `params` object or by position from a
  `params` array; positions count only the `rpc_arg` providers, so HTTP
  providers mixed into a method do not shift them. A short array leaves
  trailing parameters missing; extra elements and unknown members are
  ignored; a scalar `params` is `-32602`.
* Every request runs under a `request_scope` registered by id (namespaced
  by the MCP session when there is one). `endpoint::cancel(id)` fires its
  cancellation slot, so awaits inside the callable are interrupted; a
  cancelled JSON-RPC request answers `-32800`. `std::optional<T>` and `with_default` make a parameter
  optional. Method names are case-sensitive; registering a name twice
  throws at startup.

[heading MCP]

* Supported protocol revisions are `2025-11-25`, `2025-06-18` and
  `2025-03-26`. A request for a supported version is echoed; anything else
  is answered with the newest supported version, as the specification asks.
  A request carrying an unsupported `MCP-Protocol-Version` header is `400`.
* Stateful endpoints mint `Mcp-Session-Id` on `initialize`; later requests
  without it are `400`, with an unknown or expired id `404`, and `DELETE`
  ends the session. Stateless endpoints issue and require no id.
* JSON-RPC batches are rejected (`-32600`), following the current
  transport. A JSON-RPC response or notification posted by the client is
  acknowledged with `202`.
* Server-to-client requests (`request`, and `create_message`, `elicit`,
  `list_roots` on top of it) go out over the session's event stream (or
  stdout on stdio) with server-side ids; the client's response is matched
  by id and routed to the awaiting coroutine without taking the session
  lock, so a tool can await its own client while it holds the session.
  Other requests from the same session wait meanwhile. An error reply
  throws `client_error`; a timeout or the session ending throws
  `system_error`. `session_info` carries the client's identity and
  declared `capabilities`, so a tool can check before asking.
  `on_client_notification` hooks notifications the client sends, such as
  `notifications/roots/list_changed`.
* Prompts are registered like tools (`prompt(name, description, callable,
  mcp_arg...)`); a string result becomes one user text message, an array
  is taken as the messages, an object with `messages` is passed through.
  The `prompts` capability is advertised only when prompts exist.
* `resources/subscribe` is stateful-only (the subscription lives in the
  session); `resource_updated(uri)` notifies subscribed sessions, and the
  `*_changed()` helpers broadcast `list_changed`. Stateless endpoints
  answer subscribe with `-32601` and do not advertise it.
* `*/list` methods paginate when `server_info::page_size` is set: cursors
  are opaque strings (`-32602` when invalid), `nextCursor` is present
  while more items remain.
* `tools/call` renders scalar results as text and wraps them as
  `structuredContent: {"result": ...}`; object results are passed through
  as `structuredContent`; `void` yields an empty `content` array. Unknown
  tools and bad arguments are `-32602`; an unknown resource is `-32002`.
* Resource templates follow RFC 6570: `{var}` stops at `/`, `?`, `#`
  and `,`; `{+var}` spans them; `{#var}`, `{.var}`, `{/var}`, `{;var}`,
  `{?a,b}` and `{&a,b}` are optional when their prefix is absent, so one
  template serves `calc://x/{i}` and `calc://x/{i}?format=json`. `uri_arg`
  binds the captured values; an absent optional one is `argument_not_found`,
  which a `std::optional` or `with_default` parameter absorbs.
* `tools/list` carries an `outputSchema` derived from the callable's
  return type with the same wrapping (described structs and string maps
  as is, scalars, strings, enums and sequences under `result`), unless
  `tool_spec::output_schema` sets or disables it. `tool_spec` also adds
  `title` and `annotations` (read-only, destructive, idempotent,
  open-world hints).
* `logging/setLevel` is stored per session (initially
  `server_info::default_log_level`); `log()` and `log_all()` emit
  `notifications/message` only at or above it. The `logging` capability
  is always advertised.
* `completion/complete` consults completers registered per prompt or
  resource-template argument (`complete_prompt`, `complete_resource`);
  an argument without one answers an empty list, an unknown ref is
  `-32602`, and at most 100 values are returned with `hasMore` and
  `total`. The `completions` capability appears once a completer exists.
* The `GET` event stream is one per session (`409` for a second), fed by
  `notify`/`broadcast`; messages queued before a stream opens are kept.
  Deleting or sweeping the session ends the stream. `notify` does not
  refresh the session's idle timer. A tool reaches its own stream through
  the injected `session_info`.
* Every stream event carries an `id`; a session keeps the last
  `server_info::stream_history` events (100 by default). A `GET` with
  `Last-Event-ID` replays the newer ones before continuing live, and the
  copies still queued are skipped, so a reconnecting client sees each
  event exactly once.
* Tools return plain values (text plus `structuredContent`) or the typed
  results in `mcp/content.hpp`: `image`, `audio`, `embedded_resource`,
  `text`, or a full `tool_result` with several items and an explicit
  `isError`. A resource callable may return `blob` for binary contents;
  base64 is produced by `base64_encode` or the `from_bytes` helpers.
* `notifications/cancelled` is handled before the session lock is taken,
  so it can reach a tool that is running; the tool's awaits are
  interrupted and, as the specification asks, no response is sent for the
  cancelled request. `_meta.progressToken` on a request enables
  `request_scope::report_progress`, which emits `notifications/progress`
  on the session's stream; without a token the reports are dropped.
* The stdio transport (`serve_stdio`) runs one implicit session: created
  on `initialize`, removed at end of input. Requests before `initialize`
  are answered `-32600`. Requests run concurrently with reading (still
  serialised per session) so a cancellation can arrive mid-call. Replies
  and notifications share one writer so lines never interleave.

[heading OpenAPI]

* A route is expressible when its matcher is a conjunction of exactly one
  `target == ...` and at least one `method == ...` (several may be joined
  with `||`), plus optional header, cookie and version constraints.
  Negation, `!=` and `||` over paths are rejected at compile time rather
  than documented approximately.
* Header and cookie constraints become required parameters with a `const`
  schema. Path placeholders not bound by a `path_arg` are still declared;
  a greedy `{*name}` renders as `{name}` with a note.
* A container parameter fed by `query_arg` is a comma-separated list in
  one occurrence (`explode: false`); `query_list_arg` collects repeated
  keys, the `form`/`explode` default.
* `with_default` values render as `default`; `constrained(provider,
  {...})` renders its rules as JSON Schema keywords (`minimum`,
  `pattern`, `enum`, `minItems`, `format`, `examples`, ...) and enforces
  them at request time on every surface (`400`, `-32602`, tool error),
  so the document and the behaviour cannot drift apart.
* `add_security_scheme` fills `components.securitySchemes`;
  `set_security` and `operation::security` state requirements, and a
  route or provider reading a scheme's header, cookie or query parameter
  (or `Authorization` for an `http` scheme) is documented as that
  requirement instead of a parameter. `anonymous` emits `security: []`.
* `boost::describe`d types carry a `title` and are hoisted into
  `components.schemas`, where parameters and responses refer to them by
  JSON Reference; `set_inline_schemas` keeps them in place.
* The response schema is derived from the callable's return type when the
  built-in conversions apply; a hand-written `http::response` documents
  only a generic `200`. Per-operation `responses` override the default.
* Schemas come from `json_schema_for<T>()`: `boost::describe`d structs
  render their members (optional members are not required), described
  enums render as string enumerations, sequences and string-keyed maps
  render `items` and `additionalProperties`. MCP `inputSchema` uses the
  same function, so both documents agree by construction.

[endsect]

[endsect]
